# E-commerce Product Catalog - Cursor Rules

You are working on an e-commerce product catalog system. Always follow these rules.

## Tech Stack
- Monorepo: Turborepo + pnpm (workspaces)
- Frontend: Next.js 14 (App Router) + TypeScript + Tailwind CSS
- Backend: Fastify + TypeScript
- Database: PostgreSQL 15+ with Prisma ORM
- Cache: Redis (Upstash)
- Search: PostgreSQL Full-Text Search (tsvector + pg_trgm)
- Validation: Zod
- UI Components: shadcn/ui + Radix UI
- Icons: lucide-react
- Data fetching (client): TanStack Query
- URL state: nuqs

## Deployment
- Frontend: Vercel (Free tier)
- Backend API: Fly.io (Free tier)
- Database: Supabase (Free tier - 500MB limit)
- Redis: Upstash (Free tier - 10K commands/day)
- CDN: Cloudflare (Free)

## Code Style
- Use TypeScript strict mode (no `any`, no implicit any)
- Prefer functional components and hooks
- Use Zod for ALL input validation (API requests, env vars, forms)
- Use Prisma for DB queries; use $queryRaw only for FTS queries
- Use named exports; default exports only for Next.js pages/layouts
- Naming: camelCase for variables/functions, PascalCase for components/types/classes
- File naming: kebab-case for files, PascalCase for component files
- Always use absolute imports with path aliases (@/...)
- Prefer `const` over `let`; never use `var`
- Use async/await, not .then() chains
- Add JSDoc comments for exported functions and complex logic

## Error Handling
- Throw typed errors (custom error classes)
- Catch errors in centralized middleware (Fastify error handler)
- Never swallow errors silently
- Always log errors with context (use Pino logger)
- Return user-friendly error messages, log technical details

## Architecture Rules
- API routes: apps/api/src/routes/ (thin controllers)
- Business logic: apps/api/src/services/ (testable, pure where possible)
- DB schema: packages/db/prisma/schema.prisma
- Shared types: packages/shared-types/src/ (used by both web and api)
- React components: one component per file, co-locate with styles/tests
- Server Components by default in Next.js; "use client" only when needed

## Performance Rules
- Cache search results in Redis (5 min TTL for searches)
- Cache facets in Redis (10 min TTL)
- Cache categories tree (1 hour TTL)
- Use cursor-based pagination for infinite scroll
- Use offset pagination for traditional page UI
- Add database indexes for any new query pattern
- Use Prisma `select` to limit fields returned
- Avoid N+1 queries; use `include` or batch loading
- Use Server Components for initial data load (SSR)
- Use ISR (revalidate) for product pages

## Database Rules
- Use UUIDs for primary keys (cuid() or uuid())
- Always include createdAt and updatedAt on entities
- Use snake_case for column names (Prisma @map)
- Use camelCase in TypeScript code
- Add indexes for: foreign keys, fields used in WHERE/ORDER BY
- Use JSONB for flexible attributes (with GIN index)

## Free Tier Constraints
- Supabase: 500MB DB → keep test data ~100K products max
- Upstash Redis: 10K commands/day → cache aggressively, batch operations
- Vercel: serverless functions have cold starts → keep API on Fly.io instead
- Fly.io: 256MB RAM per VM → optimize Node.js memory usage

## When Generating Code
- Always include proper TypeScript types
- Always include error handling
- Always include input validation (Zod)
- Add helpful comments for complex logic
- Suggest tests where appropriate
- Reference @ARCHITECTURE.md for design decisions

## When Suggesting Changes
- Explain WHY before HOW
- Mention performance implications
- Mention security implications
- Suggest alternatives when relevant

## Git Conventions
- Branch naming: feature/*, fix/*, refactor/*
- Commit messages: conventional commits (feat:, fix:, chore:, docs:)
- Keep PRs small and focused
